WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.120
The lecture calls stars above about eight solar

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:07.040
masses massive. They are uncommon compared with lower-mass

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:10.500
stars. Greater core pressure and temperature allow faster

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:13.620
nuclear reactions. Although a massive star starts with

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:16.640
more fuel, its much greater luminosity uses that

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:18.880
fuel faster, giving it a shorter life.

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:22.210
Strong radiation can drive material away from a

00:00:22.210 --> 00:00:25.350
massive star. Over its lifetime, winds and outbursts

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:27.890
leave gas around it. When the star eventually

00:00:27.890 --> 00:00:30.570
explodes, the explosion can run into that previously

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:34.090
expelled material. This explains the lecture’s dirty environment:

00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:36.070
the surroundings carry a history of the star’s

00:00:36.070 --> 00:00:37.350
own mass loss.

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:42.410
If enough hydrogen-rich envelope is removed, hotter, helium-rich

00:00:42.410 --> 00:00:45.050
layers become exposed. This can produce a Wolf

00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:47.910
Rayet star. The star has changed what we

00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:50.110
can see at its surface; the lost material

00:00:50.110 --> 00:00:53.410
remains outside it. Do not identify every glowing

00:00:53.410 --> 00:00:56.310
shell as a planetary nebula. The progenitor and

00:00:56.310 --> 00:00:57.590
the physical process matter.
